I'm in plan mode right now, so I can't create the files yet — but here's exactly what Phase 1 will look like. Review it, and once you approve (or exit plan mode), I'll implement it.

## Phase 1 Plan: Read an HTML file and print it

### File 1: `page.html` (sample input)

```html
<html>
  <body>
    <h1>Hello</h1>
    <p class="intro" id="first">Welcome to AI Karyashala</p>
    <p class="note">Learn by doing</p>
  </body>
</html>
```

### File 2: `domparser.c` (the C program)

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

#define MAX_SIZE (1024 * 1024)  /* 1 MB hard limit for Phase 1 */

int main(int argc, char *argv[])
{
    if (argc < 2) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Usage: %s <html-file>\n", argv[0]);
        return EXIT_FAILURE;
    }

    FILE *fp = fopen(argv[1], "r");
    if (fp == NULL) {
        perror("Error opening file");
        return EXIT_FAILURE;
    }

    static char buffer[MAX_SIZE];
    size_t n = fread(buffer, 1, MAX_SIZE - 1, fp);
    fclose(fp);

    buffer[n] = '\0';

    if (n == 0) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Error: file is empty\n");
        return EXIT_FAILURE;
    }

    /* Print the file contents */
    printf("%s", buffer);
    if (buffer[n - 1] != '\n')
        printf("\n");

    return EXIT_SUCCESS;
}
```

### What this phase demonstrates (the "what I implemented" part)

| Piece | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `fopen(argv[1], "r")` | Takes the HTML file path from the command line and opens it |
| `perror` + usage check | Graceful errors for missing argument or nonexistent file |
| `fread` into a stack buffer | Reads the whole file into memory (1 MB safety cap — fine for Phase 1, and `static` so it lives in BSS, not the stack) |
| Null-termination | Turns the raw bytes into a proper C string |
| `printf` of the buffer | Prints file contents to stdout |
| `n == 0` check | File exists but is empty |

### Build & run

```bash
gcc -Wall -Wextra -o domparser domparser.c
./domparser page.html
```

Expected output: the contents of `page.html` echoed back exactly.

### Deliberate Phase-1 simplifications (to revisit later)
1. **Fixed buffer** — Phase 2+ can switch to dynamic allocation (grow-on-demand) when the parser needs to hold the input long-term.
2. **No parsing yet** — this just proves file I/O works end to end; tokenizer comes next phase.

Does this match what you had in mind for Phase 1? Approve and I'll write the files — or tell me any adjustments first.

